* Fischer vs. [[Boris Spassky]], [[World Chess Championship 1972]]; 6th match game, [[Queen's Gambit Declined]], [[Queen's Gambit Declined#QGD Main Variations|Tartakower Defense]] (D59), 1–0;<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1044366 |title=Robert James Fischer vs. Boris Spassky, World Championship Match (1972), Reykjavik, rd 6 |website=[[Chessgames.com]] }}</ref> [[World Chess Championship 1972#Game 6|annotated on the 1972 match page]]. Fischer called this game his best of the match.<ref>[[#Kasparov|Kasparov 2004]], p. 438.</ref> [[Efim Geller]] had told Spassky about the strong move 14...Qb7 during their preparation, but Spassky had forgotten the advice and played 14...a6. Geller won with 14...Qb7 against [[Jan Timman]] in the AVRO 1973 tournament.<ref>{{cite book |title=Avro Schaaktoernooi 1973 |first=Max |last=Euwe |date=1973 |publisher=Unieboek |language=nl |isbn=9026922205}}</ref>
* Boris Spassky vs. Fischer, World Chess Championship 1972; 13th match game, [[Alekhine Defense]], [[Alekhine Defense#Modern Variation|Modern Variation]], Alburt Variation (B04), 0–1;<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1128889 |title=Boris Spassky vs. Robert James Fischer, World Championship Match (1972), Reykjavik, rd 13 |website=Chessgames.com }}</ref> [[World Chess Championship 1972#Game 13|annotated on the 1972 match page]]. Botvinnik called this game "the highest creative achievement of Fischer". He resolved a drawish [[opposite-colored bishops endgame]] by sacrificing his bishop and trapping his own rook. "Then five passed pawns struggled with the white rook. Nothing similar had been seen before in chess."<ref>[[#Soltis2003|Soltis 2003]], p. 271.</ref>
* [[Fischer–Spassky (1992 match)|Fischer vs. Boris Spassky, 1992]]; 1st match game, [[Ruy Lopez]], [[Ruy Lopez#Breyer Variation|Breyer Variation]] (C95), 1–0;<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chessgame?gid=1129672 |title=Robert James Fischer vs. Boris Spassky, Fischer–Spassky (1992), Sveti Stefan & Belgrade, rd 1 |website=[[Chessgames.com]] }}</ref> [[Fischer–Spassky (1992 match)#Game 1|annotated on the 1992 match page]]. Fischer's "fine" victory in his first competitive game in 20 years "made a great impression on the chess world", although in Kasparov's view, Spassky's play was below the standard of the leading grandmasters of the time.<ref>[[#Kasparov|Kasparov 2004]], p. 488.</ref>

